.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CTX_SET_INFO_CALLBACK 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_CTX_set_info_callback ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_get_info_callback ,
.Nm SSL_set_info_callback ,
.Nm SSL_get_info_callback
.Nd handle information callback for SSL connections
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft void
.Fo SSL_CTX_set_info_callback
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "void (*callback) (const SSL *ssl"
.Fa "int type"
.Fa "int val)"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo 
.Fa "*SSL_CTX_get_info_callback(SSL_CTX *ctx)"
.Fc
 (const SSL *ssl, int type, int val);
.Ft void
.Fo SSL_set_info_callback
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "void (*callback) (const SSL *ssl"
.Fa "int type"
.Fa "int val)"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo 
.Fa "*SSL_get_info_callback(const SSL *ssl)"
.Fc
 (const SSL *ssl, int type, int val);
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_info_callback
sets the
.Sy callback
function, that can be used to obtain state information for SSL objects
created from
.Fa ctx
during connection setup and use.
The setting for
.Fa ctx
is overridden from the setting for a specific SSL object, if specified.
When
.Sy callback
is NULL, no callback function is used.
.Pp
.Fn SSL_set_info_callback
sets the
.Sy callback
function, that can be used to obtain state information for
.Fa ssl
during connection setup and use.
When
.Sy callback
is NULL, the callback setting currently valid for
.Fa ctx
is used.
.Fa ssl
.Sy MUST NOT
be NULL.
.Pp
.Xr SSL_CTX_get_info_callback 3
returns a pointer to the currently set information callback function for
.Fa ctx .
.Pp
.Xr SSL_get_info_callback 3
returns a pointer to the currently set information callback function for
.Fa ssl .
.Sh NOTES
When setting up a connection and during use, it is possible to obtain
state information from the SSL/TLS engine.
When set, an information callback function is called whenever a
significant event occurs such as: the state changes, an alert appears,
or an error occurs.
.Pp
The callback function is called as
.Sy callback(SSL *ssl, int where, int ret) .
The
.Sy where
argument specifies information about where (in which context) the
callback function was called.
If
.Sy ret
is 0, an error condition occurred.
If an alert is handled, SSL_CB_ALERT is set and
.Sy ret
specifies the alert information.
.Pp
.Sy where
is a bit-mask made up of the following bits:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It SSL_CB_LOOP
Callback has been called to indicate state change or some other
significant state machine event.
This may mean that the callback gets invoked more than once per state in
some situations.
.It SSL_CB_EXIT
Callback has been called to indicate exit of a handshake function.
This will happen after the end of a handshake, but may happen at other
times too such as on error or when IO might otherwise block and
nonblocking is being used.
.It SSL_CB_READ
Callback has been called during read operation.
.It SSL_CB_WRITE
Callback has been called during write operation.
.It SSL_CB_ALERT
Callback has been called due to an alert being sent or received.
.It SSL_CB_READ_ALERT (SSL_CB_ALERT|SSL_CB_READ)
.It SSL_CB_WRITE_ALERT (SSL_CB_ALERT|SSL_CB_WRITE)
.It SSL_CB_ACCEPT_LOOP (SSL_ST_ACCEPT|SSL_CB_LOOP)
.It SSL_CB_ACCEPT_EXIT (SSL_ST_ACCEPT|SSL_CB_EXIT)
.It SSL_CB_CONNECT_LOOP (SSL_ST_CONNECT|SSL_CB_LOOP)
.It SSL_CB_CONNECT_EXIT (SSL_ST_CONNECT|SSL_CB_EXIT)
.It SSL_CB_HANDSHAKE_START
Callback has been called because a new handshake is started.
It also occurs when resuming a handshake following a pause to handle
early data.
.It SSL_CB_HANDSHAKE_DONE
Callback has been called because a handshake is finished.
It also occurs if the handshake is paused to allow the exchange of early
data.
.El
.Pp
The current state information can be obtained using the
.Xr SSL_state_string 3
family of functions.
.Pp
The
.Sy ret
information can be evaluated using the
.Xr SSL_alert_type_string 3
family of functions.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_set_info_callback
does not provide diagnostic information.
.Pp
.Xr SSL_get_info_callback 3
returns the current setting.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following example callback function prints state strings,
information about alerts being handled and error messages to the
.Sy bio_err
BIO.
.Bd -literal
 void apps_ssl_info_callback(const SSL *s, int where, int ret)
 {
     const char *str;
     int w = where & ~SSL_ST_MASK;

     if (w & SSL_ST_CONNECT)
         str = "SSL_connect";
     else if (w & SSL_ST_ACCEPT)
         str = "SSL_accept";
     else
         str = "undefined";

     if (where & SSL_CB_LOOP) {
         BIO_printf(bio_err, "%s:%s\en", str, SSL_state_string_long(s));
     } else if (where & SSL_CB_ALERT) {
         str = (where & SSL_CB_READ) ? "read" : "write";
         BIO_printf(bio_err, "SSL3 alert %s:%s:%s\en", str,
                    SSL_alert_type_string_long(ret),
                    SSL_alert_desc_string_long(ret));
     } else if (where & SSL_CB_EXIT) {
         if (ret == 0) {
             BIO_printf(bio_err, "%s:failed in %s\en",
                        str, SSL_state_string_long(s));
         } else if (ret < 0) {
             BIO_printf(bio_err, "%s:error in %s\en",
                        str, SSL_state_string_long(s));
         }
     }
 }
.Ed
